'''''Salvà v. Grup Llibertat''''' (1983) was a landmark {{w|court case}} in [[Balboa]] in which [[Núria Salvà i Bauzà]] and eleven other women members of [[Grup Llibertat]], an agricultural {{w|cooperative}} in [[Jala Province|Jala]], challenged their exclusion from cooperative board elections. The court ruled in favour of Grup Llibertat, holding that cooperative bylaws were internal private contracts that the state could not modify absent a constitutional mandate. The ruling was a catalyst for the rural women's movement and directly led to the organisation of the 1984 National Women's March.

== Background ==

[[Grup Llibertat]] was one of the largest agricultural cooperatives in [[Jala Province|Jala]], with a membership of approximately 400 families. The cooperative's bylaws restricted voting membership to "heads of household," a term interpreted in practice to mean the male head of each family unit, regardless of whether the woman contributed equally to the cooperative's labour. Women who applied for voting membership were routinely rejected by the cooperative's board.

[[Núria Salvà i Bauzà]] was a member of Grup Llibertat who had worked in the cooperative's agricultural operations for over fifteen years. Salvà and eleven other women members applied for voting membership in 1982 and were rejected. The women argued that they contributed equally to the cooperative's labour and that their exclusion from governance was arbitrary and discriminatory.

== Case ==

Salvà and the eleven co-plaintiffs brought a case before the Jala Provincial Court, arguing that Grup Llibertat's exclusion of women from voting membership constituted {{w|sex discrimination}} under the general principles of the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. The plaintiffs' legal team argued that the cooperative, despite being a private organisation, performed a quasi-public function in the rural economy and that its governance structures should be subject to anti-discrimination principles.

Grup Llibertat defended its bylaws on the grounds that cooperative governance was a matter of internal private contract and that the state could not compel the modification of bylaws absent a constitutional mandate. The cooperative argued that the Twelve Guarantees' enumerated categories did not include sex and that, in any event, the Guarantees applied to state action rather than private organisations.

== Ruling ==

The court ruled in favour of Grup Llibertat. The ruling held that cooperative bylaws were internal private contracts and that the state could not compel their modification absent a {{w|constitutional amendment}} establishing sex-based protections. The court further held that the Twelve Guarantees' enumerated categories were exhaustive and that sex was not a protected category.

The ruling underscored the limits of existing law: without sex-based constitutional protections, private institutions — including cooperatives that received substantial public funding — could legally exclude women from governance. The court noted that the remedy for the plaintiffs' situation lay with the legislature, not the judiciary.

== Significance ==

The Salvà case was a catalyst for the rural women's movement in Balboa. The ruling demonstrated that the absence of constitutional protections affected not only urban professional women but also rural women's economic and political participation. The case received significant media coverage in Jala and Tramuntana, where cooperative farming was the dominant economic model.

Salvà organised the 1984 National Women's March in direct response to the ruling. The march, which drew an estimated 5,000 women from across the country to [[Sant Cristòfor]], was the largest women's demonstration in Balboan history and is widely regarded as the event that forced the National Assembly to begin serious consideration of the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sex-Based Discrimination]], which was passed in 1986.

Following the amendment, Grup Llibertat revised its bylaws to admit women to voting membership. Salvà was elected to the cooperative's board in 1987, becoming its first female member.

